+// BibleText patch: atomic preferences write.
+//
+// Stock Fyne opened the real preferences.json with os.Create — O_TRUNC — and
+// then let saveToStorage encode the whole values map into it. So from the
+// moment the writer is opened until the encoder's write lands, the reader's
+// only copy of that file is zero bytes long. Kill the process in that window
+// (force quit, an iOS jetsam, a crash, the kill an app update performs) and
+// everything in it is gone: settings, the reading position, and the shared
+// notes other people sent. A setting can be chosen again; a note exists
+// nowhere else. Worse, an emptied file is not detectably damaged — it parses
+// as "no values", so every guard downstream sees a legitimately empty store
+// and carries on. We watched a torn file turn 6 notes into 0 on the simulator.
+//
+// The window is short but it is entered constantly: this app rewrites the
+// whole blob on every chapter turn and again on every lifecycle flush, from
+// two goroutines that nothing serialises.
+//
+// The fix is the standard one: write the new blob to a temp file, then rename
+// it over the target. rename(2) is atomic — anyone opening the path (or
+// looking at it after a crash) sees the whole old file or the whole new one,
+// never a half of either. It is atomic only WITHIN a filesystem, which is why
+// the temp file is created in the target's own directory rather than in
+// os.TempDir.
+//
+// Ordering matters: the data has to be durable BEFORE the name is published,
+// or a power loss can leave a perfectly-named file full of zeroes — the exact
+// failure this set out to remove. saveToStorage already calls Sync() before
+// Close(), so that ordering falls out for free; Close only fsyncs itself when
+// nobody has since the last Write.
+//
+// Two fsync choices require measurement. The benchmarks ship beside this file
+// and measure every variant in one process because filesystem latency varies
+// too widely across separate runs for a useful direct comparison.
+//
+//  1. Close fsynced unconditionally, on the assumption that a second fsync of
+//     an already-clean file is free. It is not — on APFS os.File.Sync is
+//     fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC), a full device barrier — and it roughly doubled the
+//     cost of a save. Hence the `synced` flag.
+//  2. Close then fsynced the DIRECTORY, so that the rename itself would survive
+//     a power cut. Textbook, and here not worth it: it cost about as much again
+//     (4.1ms raw stock, 5.4ms temp+rename, 8.7ms adding the directory fsync;
+//     best of 4 runs, 300 iterations, 200-note store), and it buys nothing
+//     against the threat we actually have. A killed process — force quit,
+//     jetsam, crash, the kill an app update performs — cannot tear a rename:
+//     the page cache belongs to the kernel and outlives the process. Without
+//     the directory fsync a power cut can lose the LAST save and leave the
+//     previous complete file, which is the outcome we would choose anyway.
+//     Dropping it also drops a Windows wrinkle, where a directory handle cannot
+//     be fsynced at all.
+//
